          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em contact manifold
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            of odd
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2n+1
            which is equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential 1-form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            that is non-degenerate in the sense that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wedge+product"} wedge product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi dR
                  %mi A
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo ∧
                        %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \wedge (d_{dR} A)^{\wedge^n}
            does not vanish.
          %p
            The special case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed
            %em regular
            contact manifolds
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P,A)
            are essentially equivalent to the total spaces of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+bundles"} circle bundles
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to X
            over an
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ehresmann+connection"} Ehresmann connection
            1-form on the total space (
            %a{:href => "#BoothbyWang"}> BoothbyWang (1958)
            ).
          %p
            If in this case the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            2-form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            makes the base space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            into a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P,A)
            is a corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+circle+bundle"} prequantum circle bundle
            that provides a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+prequantization"} geometric prequantization
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : P \to P
            of a contact manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P,A)
            is called a
            %em contactomorphism
            (in analogy with
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectomorphism"} symplectomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            up to multiplication by a function. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P,A)
            is regular and hence a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            a contactomorphism that strictly preserves the connection 1-form is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism"} quantomorphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+group"} quantomorphism group
            is that of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+algebra"} Poisson algebra
            of the base symplectic manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#darboux_theorem Darboux theorem
          %p
            There is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Darboux+theorem"} Darboux theorem
            for contact structures, stating how they are locally equivalent to a standard contact structure (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Arnold78"} Arnold 78, page 362
          %h3#relation_to_principal_connections
            Relation to
            = succeed "-principal" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to X
              a smooth
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+U%281%29-bundle"} principal U(1)-bundle
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega \in \Omega^2(X)
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+2-form"} differential 2-form
              representing its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+class"} Chern class
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ehresmann+connection"} Ehresmann connection
              1-form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \Omega^1(P)
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              and such that
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                  is a regular contact form on
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi P
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reeb+vector+field"} Reeb vector field
                  of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                  generates the given
                  = succeed "-" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
                  on
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi P
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %p
              Moreover, every regular contact form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              on a closed manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              arises this way, up to rescaling by a constant.
          %p
            This is due to (
            %a{:href => "#BoothbyWang"}> Boothby-Wang 58
            ). The proof is recalled (and completed) in (
            %a{:href => "#Geiges"}> Geiges 08, theorem 7.2.4, 7.2.5
            ).
          %h2#History History
          %blockquote
            %p
              The following is taken from (
              %a{:href => "#Lin"}> Lin
              ).
          %p
            Originating in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+mechanics"} Hamiltonian mechanics
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+optics"} geometric optics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shiing-shen+Chern"} Shiing-shen Chern
            showed that the structure group of a contact manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M^{2n+1}
            can be reduced to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+group"} unitary group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(n)
            and therefore all of its odd
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+classes"} characteristic classes
            vanish. Since all the characteristic classes of a closed, orientable 3-manifold vanish, Chern in 1966 posed the questions of whether such a manifold always admits a contact structure and whether there are non-isomorphic contact structures on one manifold.
          %p
            One of the milestones in the study of contact geometry is
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Bennequin"} Bennequin
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3
            (
            %a{:href => "#Bennequin83"}> Bennequin 83
            ). Bennequin recognized that the induced singular
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foliation"} foliation
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surface"} surface
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/embedding"} embedded
            in a contact 3-manifold plays a crucial role for the classification of contact structures. This role was further explored in the work of Eliashberg, who distinguished two classes of contact structures in dimension 3, overtwisted and tight, and gave a homotopy classification for overtwisted contact structures on 3-manifolds (
            %a{:href => "#Eliashberg89"}> Eliashberg 89
            ). Eliashberg also proved that on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^3
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reeb+vector+field"} Reeb vector field
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Legendrean+submanifold"} Legendrean submanifold
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+field+theory"} symplectic field theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+contact+geometry"} generalized contact geometry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sasakian+manifold"} Sasakian manifold
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p Monographs and introductions:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hansj%C3%B6rg+Geiges"}> Hansjörg Geiges
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Contact geometry
                %em Handbook of Differential Geometry
                vol. 2 North-Holland, Amsterdam (2006) 315-382 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0307242"}> arXiv:math/0307242
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.sciencedirect.com/handbook/handbook-of-differential-geometry/vol/2/suppl/C"} ISBN:978-0-444-52052-4
            %li#Geiges
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hansj%C3%B6rg+Geiges"}> Hansjörg Geiges
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An Introduction to Contact Topology
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 109
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511611438"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511611438
            %li#Lin
              %p
                Xiao-Song Lin,
                %em An introduction to 3-dimensional contact geometry
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://math.ucr.edu/~xl/contact.pdf"}> pdf
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                John Etnyre,
                %em Introductory lectures on contact geometry
                Proc. Sympos. Pure Math.
                %strong 71
                (2003) 81-107 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://people.math.gatech.edu/~etnyre/preprints/papers/contlect.pdf"}> pdf
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Discussion in terms of (a modification of)
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-structures"} G-structures
          %ul
            %li
              Alfonso Giuseppe Tortorella, Luca Vitagliano &amp; Ori Yudilevich,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Homogeneous G-structures
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s10231-020-00972-9"} doi:10.1007/s10231-020-00972-9
          %p
            A
            = succeed "-generalization" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multisymplectic+geometry"} multisymplectic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-plectic+geometry"} n-plectic geometry
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Luca Vitagliano,
                %em L-infinity Algebras From Multicontact Geometry
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1311.2751"}> arXiv.1311.2751
                )
            %li#Arnold78
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Arnol%27d"}> Vladimir Arnol'd
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mathematical+methods+of+classical+mechanics"} Mathematical methods of classical mechanics
          %h3#characterization Characterization
          %p The observation that regular contact manifolds are prequantum circle bundles is due to
          %ul
            %li#BoothbyWang
              W. M. Boothby and H. C. Wang,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On contact manifolds
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/10.2307/1970165"} jstor:10.2307/1970165
          %p
            A modern review of this is in (
            %a{:href => "#Geiges"}> Geiges, section 7.2
            ).
          %p An analogous result for a weaker form of regularity is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              C. Thomas,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Almost regular contact manifolds
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.jdg/1214433722"} euclid:jdg/1214433722
          %p
            A characterization of
            = succeed "-invariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+bundles"} circle bundles
            is in
          %ul
            %li#DingGeiges
              Fan Ding,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hansj%C3%B6rg+Geiges"} Hansjörg Geiges
              = succeed "," do
                %em Contact structures on principal circle bundle
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1107.4948"} arXiv:1107.4948
          %p
            For the special case of 2-dimensional base manifolds (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 1
            ) this was obtained before in
          %ul
            %li
              R. Lutz,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Structures de contact sur les fibrés principaux en cercles de dimension trois
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              John Bland, Tom Duchamp,
              %em The Group of Contact Diffeomorphisms for Compact Contact Manifolds
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1007.2036"}> arXiv:1007.2036
              )
          %h3#further Further
          %ul
            %li#Bennequin83
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Bennequin"}> Daniel Bennequin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Entrelacements et equations de Pfaff
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/article/AST_1983__107-108__87_0.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Eliashberg89
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yakov+Eliashberg"}> Yakov Eliashberg
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Classification of overtwisted contact structures on 3-manifolds
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/download?doi=10.1.1.465.8237&rep=rep1&type=pdf"} pdf
            %li#Eliashberg92
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yakov+Eliashberg"}> Yakov Eliashberg
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Contact 3-manifolds twenty years since J Martinet’s work
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/10.5802/aif.1288.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hansj%C3%B6rg+Geiges"}> Hansjörg Geiges
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Contact structures on 1-connected 5-manifolds
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hansj%C3%B6rg+Geiges"}> Hansjörg Geiges
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Contact structures on
                    = succeed "-connected" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi n
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
                    = succeed "-manifolds" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn 2
                            %mi n
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2n+1)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hansj%C3%B6rg+Geiges"}> Hansjörg Geiges
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Constructions of contact manifolds
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hansj%C3%B6rg+Geiges"}> Hansjörg Geiges
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Normal contact structures on 3-manifolds
              %p Tôhoku Math. J. 49 (1997), 415-422.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hansj%C3%B6rg+Geiges"}> Hansjörg Geiges
                , J. Gonzalo,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Moduli of contact circles
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Contact geometry and complex surfaces
